Measure a memory hierarchy under a declared cost model (@computing-memory-cost: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-private-state-shared-pages-and-complete-service-effects.lingua#r_0044D31C76AB50C657C6A092BB") { r_008EC6006BF0912737390C8789
Correct translation and protection do not determine how long an access takes. A hierarchy can retain nearby or recently used data in smaller faster storage, while translations may also have their own cache. A data-cache miss and an absent-page event are different: one asks a lower storage level for data under an existing access, the other concerns a mapping/residency rule. Our upcoming cost example models only a data-address trace, separately from the process service simulator.

Assume a read-only, physically addressed, direct-mapped cache with two lines and one word per line. Initially both are invalid. Physical address a selects line i=a mod 2 and tag q=floor(a/2). A matching valid tag is a hit; otherwise load that word/tag and count a miss. The reference memory is fixed during this trace, so there is no write policy or coherence problem in the example. These restrictions matter; the policy is not a description of every real cache.

Suppose lookup costs one unit and a miss adds five units. Every access pays the lookup; a miss costs six in total. For N accesses and M misses, total cost is N+5M and average cost is 1+5M/N when N>0. Without the cache, assume a direct reference-memory access costs five units, giving 5N. The modeled cache is faster exactly when M/N<4/5. The empty trace costs zero and has no average per-access cost. The units are invented accounting units, not nanoseconds or measured CPU cycles.

Original trace 4,5,4,5,6,4,5,4 has four misses and four hits, costing 28 units instead of 40 direct units. Trace 4,6,4,6,4,6,4,6 repeatedly conflicts in line zero: eight misses cost 48 units, worse than direct access. Equal instruction/reference counts need not imply equal cost. A claim that more capacity or a different replacement policy helps must be evaluated against its changed costs and the actual trace, not a slogan that caching always speeds things up.

The address context also matters. A cache keyed only by virtual address could confuse process 0's virtual 0 with process 1's virtual 0 unless it incorporates the necessary context or uses a suitable invalidation/tagging design. Our small cost model instead takes physical addresses and makes no claim about translation-cache implementation. The baseline process example gives physical 4 and 8 at those same-number virtual addresses.

**Reading and work:** [Computation Structures — MIT 6.004 Spring 2017](../sources/Computation-Structures-MIT-6004.lingua#r_08E48B371DEF44C3F62A70A8CB), selected topic 14 hit/miss and policy discussion, with topic 16's translation context. Independently predict the tags after each reference in both traces. Prove the cost formula and break-even criterion, then design two traces of the same length with different locality. Full hardware timing, write-back, multi-core coherence, translation caches and measured system performance remain later work.

